<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malayalam language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malayalam language is a Dravidian language spoken mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshadweep">Lakshadweep</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, by about 35 million people. It developed from the western dialects of Old Tamil and emerged as a distinct literary language between the 9th and 13th centuries, absorbing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words. It is written in its own script, derived from Grantha, and is recognised as one of the classical languages of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malayalam-language.jpg" alt="Malayalam language" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nyilvoskt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malayalam_language_(ml,_Q36236)_distribution.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
